The board has invited Nepal to submit a new funding request under the Barbados Implementation Modalities, the fund's start-up phase, and Nepal must prepare a proposal and go through existing approval processes before it can use the money.

The $20m is the most the fund can currently give for any single disaster, making up only a small fraction of Nepal’s losses. Early official estimates put the cost of reconstruction at more than $4.7bn (£3.5bn). Nepal wrote to the fund in August, less than a week after the flood.

The board set that limit when it chose to start paying out in a test phase, a board member explained to The Independent last month, using the money it has, instead of waiting years to design a full long-term model.

if (document.cookie.split(';').some(cookie => cookie.trim() === '__DEBUG__=true')) { console.log('Ad logs: "taboola-carousel-thumbnails", injectedAtParagraph: 6'); }

"It's a very small amount compared to what we lost," Maheshwar Dhakal, a senior official in Nepal's climate and environment ministry, told AP. He said the money still sent a message.

"This shows that the global community can take the right decision at the right time to help those who are most vulnerable to climate change," he said. The government had received around $102m (£77m) from domestic and international donors by the end of September, he said, and is working with India, China and multilateral development banks to raise more.

open image in gallery {"id":"trigger-autogallery-242539","index":0} Damaged houses lie partially submerged in sludge along the banks of the Budhi Gandaki river at Soti Bazar in Nepal's Gorkha district ( AFP/Getty )

The fund currenly only holds $822m (£616m) in pledges, but only around $350m (£262m) is currently earmarked for disbursement, against 180 applications from developing countries worth a combined $2.8bn (£2.1bn).

