The money was issued for the disaster that began on 26 August when part of a mountain collapsed on Nepal's border with China's Tibet region, and more than 5,000 people are still missing. It affected about 60,000 people from 15,000 families and caused losses estimated at $2.7bn (£2bn), including to roads, bridges, schools and 13 hydropower plants, according to official data.

