No country had invoked the fund’s emergency procedures before.

The process is meant to move an application to payment in six to eight weeks, compared with six to eight months under the fund’s normal rules. The board was not due to meet until December, but it called its first extraordinary meeting after eight developing country members asked for one.

The board meets again in December to consider more requests pending with it.

open image in gallery {"id":"trigger-autogallery-242540","index":1} A drone view shows houses covered in mud after the flash floods and mudslide, at Devighat, Nuwakot district, Nepal ( Reuters )

Harjeet Singh, global convenor of the Fill the Fund campaign, which pushes governments to pay into the fund, called the decision historic but said it could not be a one-off.

"No country should have to endure a catastrophe and wait seven weeks, through historic letters and relentless pressure, just to unlock the Fund's first-ever emergency meeting," he said.

He said developed countries backed the allocation but "kept insisting it must not set a precedent and must not prejudge the fund's long-term model".

"Nepal needs billions to rebuild, and this fund is responding only in millions," he said.

"Nepal is investing its scarce resources in climate adaptation, mitigation, and resilience, yet climate finance often deepens its debt burden. For every $1 received as a grant, Nepal receives $6 in loans. Countries that contributed least to this crisis should not have to borrow to survive it. These loans divert resources from health, education, and livelihoods, burdening future generations. Meanwhile, the Fund for Responding to Loss and Damage remains terribly underfunded," said Arnab Chaudhary of the Aalawya Foundation in Nepal.

Nepal's prime minister, Balendra Shah, told the UN General Assembly in September: "We did not create this crisis, yet we are paying a colossal price for it."

A World Weather Attribution analysis that month found that warming had thinned the region's glaciers and thawed the frozen ground that holds mountain rock together, weakening the slope that collapsed.

"The headlines have moved on, but thousands are still missing in the Rasuwa and Nuwakot regions," said Dikshya Subedi, founder of the youth group Youth Action For Sustainable and Eco Nepal, whose husband's home was washed away, said.

"Entire villages are still buried under mud, and people lack the support to rebuild their homes."

The decision comes before the next UN climate talks, which begin in Turkey next month, where finance for countries hit by climate disasters is expected to be a key issue.

Aid budgets are shrinking, with official development assistance from major donors falling 23 per cent in 2025, according to the OECD, and the UK among the countries that cut aid.

"Rich nations can't show up at COP31 with empty hands: they need to deliver on climate finance," said Savio Carvalho, head of campaigns and networks at 350.org.
